WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.300
Mariana Castro: That coat
must be the most impractical

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.450
piece of archaeology gear that I've seen.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.350
But, you know, it looks good.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.330
My name is Mariana Castro,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.882
I'm an archeologist at
New York University,

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.463
and today we're going to be looking at

00:00:17.463 --> 00:00:21.245
archaeology adventure scenes
to see how real they are.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.410
We all try to dress like Indiana Jones,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.368
but nobody looks that good.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.010
Booby traps don't tend to be

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.833
very common archaeological finds.

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:49.860
There is this misconception
that in the pyramids,

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.053
there were a lot of booby
traps, but not really.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.360
So, yeah, the suspense is real. [laughs]

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.400
We do make that face. [laughs]

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.840
When we find something
really, really special.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.898
Why does he have a bag of sand?

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.160
Well, archaeology is the study of things,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.060
of what people left behind.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.980
And those things only have meaning

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.560
in a specific context.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.380
And if you remove the
context from an artifact,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.960
as looters do, and that's
really the problem,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.870
you basically lose

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:30.183
a lot of the value of that artifact.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.270
Yeah, so, if Indiana Jones
was a real archaeologist,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.930
he would have stopped and
analyzed these booby traps,

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.030
because, honestly,
they're much more unique

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:44.333
and special than the idol.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.070
Indiana: Give me the whip.

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.290
Mariana: OK, just gonna be very clear.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.317
Archaeologists do not carry bullwhips.

00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:55.490
Satipo: Throw me the idol.

00:01:58.321 --> 00:01:59.404
No time to argue.

00:01:59.404 --> 00:02:01.212
Throw me the idol, I throw you the whip!

00:02:01.212 --> 00:02:02.850
Mariana: Well, just the fact of throwing

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.290
an ancient object like that.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.180
I mean, let's start at
the very basics, right.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.780
Oh, my. [laughs]

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.246
No, don't tell any
conservator you did that.

00:02:28.096 --> 00:02:31.480
That whole part is kind
of unbelievable. [laughs]

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.550
And, I mean, a system that is probably

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.210
thousands of years old is not

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.130
going to be working like that.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.180
There were things that
were much more efficient

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.850
in keeping looters out.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.130
For example, curses.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.710
Writing down curses on the tomb chamber,

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.400
or in the entrance of the tomb,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.069
actually was very effective

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.625
in detracting people from entering.

00:03:00.238 --> 00:03:02.880
You know what, I wish my life

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.852
was a little bit more like this. [laughs]

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.790
But it's not, unfortunately.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.351
Maybe two?

00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:12.160
Ben: In this kind of
terrain, with nothing more

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.718
than dogsleds and on foot,

00:03:14.718 --> 00:03:15.859
can you imagine?

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.750
Mariana: Mapping devices are
heavily used in archaeology.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:24.430
You don't do anything
without a computer or a GPS.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.440
Riley: Assuming Ben's theory is correct

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.568
and my tracking model's accurate,

00:03:28.568 --> 00:03:31.150
we should be getting very close.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:35.300
Mariana: Reconstructing
the location of something?

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.960
You would need to be able
to program that thing

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.300
to incredibly specific parameters.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.550
The wind, the currents, the ice breaks.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.574
In this case, it would be impossible.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.781
[metal detector beeping]

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.860
Every time I see a metal detector,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.760
it's kind of like this alarm that goes

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.260
in the head of every archaeologist.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.330
Basically, metal detectors
are not commonly used.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.208
Because you don't want to constrain

00:04:06.208 --> 00:04:08.490
the results of the excavation;

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.500
you don't want to focus on just one thing.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.780
Normally, you want to lay out your trench,

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.440
and you're going to treat every
piece of evidence equally.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.618
But, you know, the majority of

00:04:18.618 --> 00:04:20.320
archaeological finds are not metal.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.400
Shaw: How could a ship
wind up way out here?

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.420
Riley: Well, I'm no
expert, but it could be

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.120
that the hydrothermic properties

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.390
of this region produce
hurricane-force ice storms

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.055
that caused the ocean to freeze

00:04:34.055 --> 00:04:36.468
and then melt and then refreeze,

00:04:36.468 --> 00:04:40.725
resulting in a semisolid
migrating landmass.

00:04:40.725 --> 00:04:43.540
Mariana: Meteorologists
even have a hard time

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.620
predicting the weather for the week,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.844
so I'm not sure if you could predict

00:04:47.844 --> 00:04:50.200
where the ship has been since it wrecked

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.473
300 years ago or however long it was.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.950
His methodology is
definitely not believable.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:05.483
We do use pickaxes, a lot. [laughs]

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.866
But we're not that anxious

00:05:09.866 --> 00:05:12.120
to get to it.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.570
I hope I don't look like
that when I'm pickaxing.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.210
And we certainly do
not find the best thing

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.243
of the whole excavation

00:05:27.243 --> 00:05:28.843
in the first 10 seconds.

00:05:28.843 --> 00:05:32.010
That is very unlikely.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.332
Maybe you get it in,
you know, the last week,

00:05:34.332 --> 00:05:35.390
if you're lucky.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:38.020
The way he gets to that bell

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.430
is probably much more akin
to how looters excavate

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:42.893
than archaeologists.

00:05:50.274 --> 00:05:52.530
So, it makes sense that
the ship is well preserved

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.900
because it was found
in ice and kept in ice

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.200
for hundreds of years, and ice is

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.370
a stable environment for most materials.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.470
I am going to give this clip a four.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.490
That coat must be the most impractical

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:12.990
archaeology gear that I've seen.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.749
But, you know, it looks good.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.530
They are actually speaking Russian.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:26.672
It's almost required for
archaeologists to know

00:06:26.672 --> 00:06:29.040
at least one language that is relevant

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.563
to their area of studies.

00:06:38.517 --> 00:06:40.700
So, the part of the computer breaking

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.020
was the most accurate
part of the clip so far.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.820
Because it does happen all the time.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.480
I have never seen archaeologists
using a glow stick.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.011
Headlamp, a thousand times more practical.

00:07:06.777 --> 00:07:09.270
Bet it was one of her tasks in the game

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.340
to ring the bell, and then it explodes.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:15.290
Yeah, sounds very gamelike.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.070
Nobody wants it to be
completely realistic,

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.730
that'd be really boring.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.868
I would rate this one also a two.

00:07:26.022 --> 00:07:28.050
Brock: OK, Lewis, drop down to B deck.

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.680
Mariana: This is an interesting example

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.150
because a lot of this initial footage

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:35.200
was actually shot at the original site.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.216
It's 100% realistic

00:07:37.216 --> 00:07:40.332
because it's exactly what they saw.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.780
Depending on whatever
circumstances you find

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:51.840
at the bottom of the ocean,
in Titanic, in this case,

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.210
some things can preserve very well,

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.710
and some things can disappear.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:01.486
Depends on oxygen, light,
conditions, currents.

00:08:01.486 --> 00:08:03.180
Brock: Make your turn, make
your turn, watch the wall.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.570
Man: Yeah, Brock, we're
at the piano, you copy?

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:09.404
Mariana: It wouldn't be
the most ridiculous thing

00:08:09.404 --> 00:08:12.230
to imagine that the piano, for example,

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:15.680
which is made of wood and
maybe ivory, I don't know,

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.730
preserved, especially under, you know,

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.413
it's a very short time since it sunk.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.283
Lewis: Baby, are you seeing this, boss?

00:08:25.283 --> 00:08:26.710
Brock: It's payday, boys.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.242
Mariana: A lot of the expeditions that

00:08:28.242 --> 00:08:30.075
archaeologists are involved in

00:08:30.075 --> 00:08:32.283
are funded by private individuals.

00:08:32.283 --> 00:08:33.770
And there's nothing wrong with that.

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:35.936
The problem starts

00:08:35.936 --> 00:08:38.427
if the private individuals want to keep

00:08:38.427 --> 00:08:40.992
the things that you are researching.

00:08:40.992 --> 00:08:42.750
You need to return whatever you excavate

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.360
to the country of origin.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.920
We don't get to keep anything, ever.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.580
That's a really, it's probably

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:50.240
the most common question that I get.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:51.910
"Do you get to keep any gold?"

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.110
No. [laughs]

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:57.020
We don't get any monetary benefit

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:59.620
that is proportional to
the things that we find.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.357
I would rate this clip, in particular,

00:09:03.357 --> 00:09:06.060
based on all the documentary evidence

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.945
that is included in the movie, eight.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.262
Herman: Indiana?

00:09:12.262 --> 00:09:13.095
Indiana?

00:09:13.095 --> 00:09:14.287
Indiana: Shh!

00:09:20.651 --> 00:09:22.929
It's the Cross of Coronado.

00:09:22.929 --> 00:09:25.300
Cortés gave it to him in 1520.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.630
Mariana: I don't see why
a teenager would be able

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:31.567
to identify a lost object.

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:33.427
Indiana: That cross is
an important artifact.

00:09:33.427 --> 00:09:35.115
It belongs in a museum.

00:09:35.115 --> 00:09:37.365
Mariana: This very cinematic experience

00:09:37.365 --> 00:09:38.773
of us versus them,

00:09:38.773 --> 00:09:40.060
and one side is the archaeologist

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.870
and the other side is the bad looter,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.840
makes sense in cinematic terms,

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.700
but in reality, it is not
the archaeologist's job

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.053
to go after the looters.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.190
Just from, for the sake of appearances,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.340
putting things in your
pocket is probably also

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.980
one of those 101 archaeology lessons.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.200
Don't put things in your pocket.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.180
It's not our job to be
vindictive against looters,

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:09.670
that's the job of the law.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.570
The idea of what you do in the field

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:14.640
and how you sensitize
people when you're working

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.080
to what you're doing.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:17.980
I think people understand
that that's their heritage,

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.110
it's their responsibility
to also protect it.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:23.210
So, your role as an
archaeologist is a lot more

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.670
soft diplomacy, in that sense,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:29.503
than it is actually going
after looters with guns.

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:34.153
Although, I should say,

00:10:34.153 --> 00:10:36.094
it was still extremely inspiring

00:10:36.094 --> 00:10:37.080
for a lot of people.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.690
He has inspired a whole
generation of archaeologists.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:43.890
So, I would probably rate this clip

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.773
1.5.

00:10:45.773 --> 00:10:48.310
'Cause it's even less likely that a child

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:49.943
would do something like this.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:57.903
Dirk: Gentlemen!

00:10:57.903 --> 00:10:59.227
May I introduce to you,

00:10:59.227 --> 00:11:02.060
after a 772-year engagement

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.727
on the bottom of the ocean.

00:11:03.727 --> 00:11:05.200
Mariana: An Egyptian sarcophagus?

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.460
With 700 years?

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:07.700
That sounds very strange.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.950
Sandecker: The king meets
the people at the museum

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:10.880
in five hours.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:12.310
Al: Don't worry, sir, he'll be there.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.970
Hey, Dirk, get your butt up here

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.210
so we can scrape some of
the crap off this thing.

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:17.651
Mariana: I think five hours is

00:11:17.651 --> 00:11:19.509
the time that it would take

00:11:19.509 --> 00:11:22.450
to get the sarcophagus to land. [laughs]

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:25.010
I think probably just the whole trip

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.690
and unpacking the
sarcophagus from the ship

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:29.200
would take five hours.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.470
'Cause look at the size of that thing.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:32.823
It would never get to
the museum in five hours.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:34.930
That would never happen.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:36.860
As we saw when he was
coming out of the ocean,

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.760
it was full of incrustations

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.176
of shells, and little animals

00:11:42.176 --> 00:11:44.134
from the bottom of the ocean, algae.

00:11:44.134 --> 00:11:46.940
That takes, again, so you
don't damage the object,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.070
it takes a long time to remove them.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:53.200
You don't just scrape them...[laughs]

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.770
away on the spot.

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:59.130
Sandecker: This, ladies and
gentlemen, is what we do.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.130
Mariana: I don't know,
did they wax the thing?

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:07.030
Because obviously, in five hours,

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:08.890
it wouldn't look like this at all

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.140
unless it's a fake that they replaced it.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.250
There's a whole conservation process

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:15.130
that you need to go through

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:19.640
before even displaying it in a museum.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.720
It would just be completely unrealistic.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.010
It takes months to desalinize things

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.100
that come from the bottom of the ocean.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.246
I would probably rate this clip a three.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:39.000
And this is an actual
excavation, for sure.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.250
This doesn't seem like a set.

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.050
This doesn't seem like a set at all.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:48.380
You can see that they
are digging trenches,

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:52.920
and everything is done
in a confined square.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:54.420
They would dig profiles where you can have

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.830
a very straight wall that shows you

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:02.470
the stratigraphy, the
layering, of your trench.

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.540
Because what you just dug out is equal

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.110
to what you see on your walls.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.426
And then you draw that
out, and you can assign

00:13:09.426 --> 00:13:12.530
artifacts that you found in those layers,

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:13.640
and you can date them.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:16.531
Whatever is above probably came

00:13:16.531 --> 00:13:18.490
after what's underneath.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:20.820
Basically stratigraphy is just a way

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:24.630
of dating your trench

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.470
and the material that comes from it.

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.691
What comes later, what comes before.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:37.368
That is a sieve.

00:13:39.089 --> 00:13:41.720
And what they're doing is probably just

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:43.880
dumping the earth that they're excavating

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.340
in the sieve, sieving the things,

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:50.810
and sorting out what was in that bucket.

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.568
That's a lot of things for one bucket,

00:13:52.568 --> 00:13:53.743
but it is possible.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.310
They're able to say that
it's not from the same period

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.960
because that's a coin or a medallion.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:11.500
Archaeologists that are
really good at what they do,

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.850
they're able to see something

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.550
and see this is out of place.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:17.736
This doesn't belong in this period.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:35.480
Even the little demon caked in earth

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:36.880
was a really nice touch because

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.005
that's exactly how they
come out of the earth.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:49.040
Actually, dogs fighting
[laughs] is a big part

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.973
of what I see when I'm on digs.

00:14:58.338 --> 00:15:00.780
If we take the part where he's having this

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:04.731
supernatural connection with the gargoyle,

00:15:04.731 --> 00:15:07.885
I would say, because of that,

00:15:07.885 --> 00:15:10.210
I will give it a nine out of 10.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.747
That would be my face too.

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.210
Daniel: Where'd you find this?

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:31.200
Catherine: Giza Plateau, 1928.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.110
Mariana: Giza is the
place where the pyramids,

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.183
the famous Giza Pyramids are located.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.534
Daniel: I've never seen
anything like this.

00:15:39.534 --> 00:15:40.800
Catherine: Of course you haven't.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.230
Mariana: Me neither.

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:44.390
Meyers: Now, there's two
lines of hieroglyphs.

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:46.390
The inner track has the classic figures,

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:49.710
but the outer track is like
the cartouche in the center.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:52.361
It's got writing unlike
we've ever found before.

00:15:55.345 --> 00:15:57.350
Daniel: Who the hell translated this?

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.780
Mariana: A common comment. [laughs]

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:00.963
In our field.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.230
Daniel: Stargate.

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:09.980
Mariana: We don't study aliens.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.690
We just assume that people built

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.210
the things we're excavating.

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:17.610
It's very problematic
when you start to assign

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:19.968
wonders of humanity

00:16:19.968 --> 00:16:22.751
to an external source of power.

00:16:22.751 --> 00:16:25.760
Credit where credit is due. [laughs]

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:26.990
Daniel: Why is the military so interested

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:29.320
in 5,000-year-old Egyptian tablets?

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.817
Jack: My report says 10,000.

00:16:31.817 --> 00:16:33.140
Mariana: It's not uncommon
for archaeologists

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:34.970
and the military to work together.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.794
The main reason, normally,

00:16:36.794 --> 00:16:40.569
is that the archaeological
site you want to excavate

00:16:40.569 --> 00:16:42.370
is in a military site

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:44.855
or a military-protected area.

00:16:44.855 --> 00:16:47.440
In these situations, whatever you do

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:49.810
requires the permission of the military,

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:52.010
so in a way they are above you.

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.618
They're not the only ones.
Normally you also have

00:16:53.618 --> 00:16:55.343
the department of antiquities

00:16:55.343 --> 00:16:57.253
of whatever country you're excavating in.

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:00.160
Daniel: This figure, 10,000, is ludicrous.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.980
I mean, Egyptian culture
didn't even exist.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.284
Shore: We know.

00:17:03.284 --> 00:17:05.430
But the sonic and radiocarbon...

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:06.660
Mariana: What in the world? [laughs]

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.827
I mean, radiocarbon is a thing

00:17:09.827 --> 00:17:13.550
and very important for
archaeologists to date things,

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.190
organic materials,

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.270
but I have no idea what sonic dating is.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.270
Hiring an archaeologist
when you do have an issue

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.730
understanding something from the past,

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:25.700
and maybe the secret military involvement,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:27.670
but I can't comment on
that 'cause it's secret,

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:29.003
I'm gonna give it a four.

00:17:30.359 --> 00:17:33.640
Rick: Are you sure we can find
the secret compartment thing?

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:34.781
Mariana: You wouldn't be underneath

00:17:34.781 --> 00:17:36.347
the thing you wanna open,

00:17:36.347 --> 00:17:39.690
that's just the recipe for disaster.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:43.000
I would not be playing golf
in an ancient Egyptian city.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:47.220
Evelyn: Oh, and you know how
they took out your brains?

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.030
Jonathan: Evy, I don't
think we need to know this.

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:51.288
Evelyn: They take a sharp red-hot poker,

00:17:51.288 --> 00:17:52.521
stick it up your nose.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.120
Mariana: Anytime in archaeology
there's an enclosed space,

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.703
you have to wear those construction hats.

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:05.681
Evelyn: Oh, my God, it's a...

00:18:05.681 --> 00:18:07.456
it's a sarcophagus.

00:18:09.854 --> 00:18:11.840
Rick: This looks like
some sort of a clock.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.530
Mariana: You can see here
several types of methodologies.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.860
You can use the brush,
you can use the hand,

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:19.170
you can blow.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.050
If it's something this important,

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:23.290
you would probably go really, really slow,

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.690
because there could be
paint on it, for example.

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:33.750
So, first of all, these
things are unbelievably heavy.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:36.750
So I'm not sure how they transported them.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.860
As an archaeologist, you very rarely treat

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:41.610
or open

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.235
or try to crack things on site.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:57.100
I'm not an Egyptologist,
but from what I've seen,

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:00.650
wouldn't just be one layer of protection

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:02.000
of the mummified body.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.700
Inside a stone coffin, you
would have a wooden coffin.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.370
I am going to rate this clip a two.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:16.287
Milo: Hey, wait a minute! Who are you?

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:29.390
Mariana: After all the work you put in,

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:30.481
physically and mentally,

00:19:30.481 --> 00:19:33.306
and all the crazy
research that you've done,

00:19:33.306 --> 00:19:35.520
and then you finally get to the site

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.250
and you discover something
that means something to you.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.933
This is actually

00:19:39.933 --> 00:19:42.016
a really good illustration
of that feeling.

00:19:42.016 --> 00:19:44.150
That sense of wonder, because it's

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:45.833
a real connection with the past.

00:19:48.370 --> 00:19:50.570
OK, this is my dream. [laughs]

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:52.550
I grew up with this movie, so in a way,

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:55.940
I always kind of imagined
that archaeology at least

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.040
would feel like this big discovery,

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.483
and sometimes it does.

00:20:01.627 --> 00:20:03.691
Audrey: It's beautiful.

00:20:03.691 --> 00:20:05.631
Joshua: Milo, I gotta hand it to you.

00:20:05.631 --> 00:20:06.610
Really came through.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:08.259
Mariana: It's not one of the most

00:20:08.259 --> 00:20:09.967
popular Disney movies,

00:20:09.967 --> 00:20:12.970
but a lot of people that
went into archaeology

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.750
that I've met also reference this movie

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:17.340
as one of their starting points.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.990
Because it's a cartoon, it's
appropriate for children,

00:20:19.990 --> 00:20:21.820
it taps into this imaginary,

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:25.120
this wonderful thing that
is to study the past.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.130
Based on how realistic this clip is

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:31.410
in capturing the feeling of what it means

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:34.260
to be an archaeologist in
those light-bulb moments,

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:36.260
I'm gonna give it a 10.

